<chapter xml:id="ch04-the-derivative" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Derivative</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>The previous chapter ended with one central limit:</p>
    <md>\lim_{h\to 0}\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h}.</md>
    <p>The numerator measures change in output, and the denominator measures change in input. The quotient is an average rate of change. The limit, if it exists, is the instantaneous rate of change.</p>
    <p>That limit now becomes a new mathematical object: the <term>derivative</term>. It is the mathematical version of what a speedometer registers, and it represents the slope of a curve when we zoom in until the graph appears straight.</p>
  </introduction>

